<Base title="Methodology">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p>Termsinator locates public terms, privacy policies, cookie policies, acceptable-use rules, subscription terms, and AI or data-use policies. Retrieved text is normalized and hashed. Reports identify the document revision and provide short exact citations.</p>

  <h2>Evaluation</h2>
  <p>Each model evaluates the same document bundle using the same versioned matrix. The matrix covers data collection, sharing and tracking, retention and rights, content and AI use, account control, payments, disputes, and transparency.</p>

  <h2>Grades</h2>
  <p>Public results use broad letter grades: A (strongest user protections), B, C, D, and E (highest concern). Criteria are evaluated on a zero-to-four rubric internally, then the server applies published weights and critical-risk caps before assigning the grade. A letter grade avoids implying that small numeric differences are precise or reproducible.</p>
  <p>Grades do not make automated interpretation certain. Evidence coverage, citations, model disagreement, and repeatability remain visible. Insufficient source coverage produces an ungraded result rather than a misleading grade.</p>

  <h2>Multiple models</h2>
  <p>Every model result is shown. The aggregate uses the median internal result for each criterion and then recalculates categories and the final grade. Grade ranges, applicability conflicts, and minority critical warnings remain visible.</p>

  <h2>Classification</h2>
  <p>Services are classified by offering type, sector, subcategory, monetization, audience, and data sensitivity. Classification is separate from the risk score.</p>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <p>The analysis covers public, logged-out documents under the jurisdiction assumptions shown in the report. It may not cover terms displayed during signup, inside applications, or only in particular countries. Automated analysis is not legal advice.</p>
</Base>
